          'Tonal melody is grammatical: it parses into units with beginnings, middles and punctuated ends. The motive is the smallest meaningful unit — the four-note opening of Beethoven’s Fifth Symphony is the canonical example — and composers spin whole movements from motives through repetition, transposition, sequence (repetition at successively higher or lower pitch levels), inversion (upside-down), and fragmentation (breaking the motive into smaller cells). The phrase is the basic complete thought, typically four or eight bars in Classical style, ending with a cadence — a conventional melodic-harmonic formula of closure analogous to a comma or period.',
          'Two archetypes organize most Classical themes. The period pairs an antecedent phrase ending with a weak cadence (a musical comma, usually a half cadence on V) with a consequent phrase that answers, often with the same opening, and closes more strongly (an authentic cadence on I); the opening of Mozart’s Piano Sonata in A major, K. 331, is a textbook parallel period. The sentence, by contrast, states a basic idea, repeats it (exactly or varied), then drives through fragmentation and acceleration to a cadence, in a characteristic 2+2+4 proportion; the opening of Beethoven’s Piano Sonata Op. 2 No. 1 in F minor is the classic case. Hearing which archetype is in play tells a performer where the phrase is going and where to breathe.',
