          'Solmization attaches syllables to pitches so that the ear can name what it hears. In the movable-do system used in this course, do is always the tonic, so the syllables encode function: sol is always the dominant regardless of key, and ti always leans toward do. Fixed-do, standard in French and Italian conservatories, instead assigns do permanently to C, making the syllables letter names rather than functions; scale-degree numbers (1 through 7) work like movable-do with digits. We choose movable-do because Year 1 is about tonal function, and the system makes every melody in every major key the same solfège problem.',
